def merge3(base, ours, theirs):
    merged, conflicts = {}, []
    for path in sorted(set(base) | set(ours) | set(theirs)):
        b, o, t = base.get(path), ours.get(path), theirs.get(path)
        if o == t:
            result = o
        elif o == b:
            result = t
        elif t == b:
            result = o
        else:
            conflicts.append(path)
            result = o
        if result is not None:
            merged[path] = result
    return merged, conflicts

exercises.solution:
```python
def merge3(base, ours, theirs):
    merged, conflicts = {}, []
    for path in sorted(set(base) | set(ours) | set(theirs)):
        b, o, t = base.get(path), ours.get(path), theirs.get(path)
        if o == t:
            result = o
        elif o == b:
            result = t
        elif t == b:
            result = o
        else:
            conflicts.append(path)
            result = o
        if result is not None:
            merged[path] = result
    return merged, conflicts
```

Representing a missing file as `None` turns additions and deletions into ordinary changes: "deleted on our side, untouched on theirs" is just `o != b` and `t == b`, so we take `o`, which is `None`, and the file disappears. The order of tests matters only for the first case: when both sides agree, there is nothing to decide, even if both differ from the base. In real Git, the conflict case would go on to a line-level three-way merge of the file's contents, applying the same rule to regions of lines; and because unchanged subtrees have equal hashes, Git skips whole directories without opening them.
